            "## TIME, SPEED AND DISTANCE",
            "The fundamental relationship between time, speed, and distance underpins most in-flight navigation calculations: Distance = Speed x Time, which can be rearranged to solve for whichever value is unknown, Speed = Distance ÷ Time, or Time = Distance ÷ Speed. In practice, this is most often applied using groundspeed (the aircraft's actual speed over the ground, accounting for wind) to calculate an estimated time en-route (ETE) or estimated time of arrival (ETA) for a given leg or the overall flight.",
            "A pilot must be comfortable performing these calculations both using a flight computer (mechanical or electronic) and through basic mental arithmetic or simple written working, since exam questions and real-world flying alike frequently require a quick, confident calculation without necessarily reaching for a specific tool. A commonly useful shortcut: at any given groundspeed in knots, the aircraft covers that many nautical miles in exactly 60 minutes, so distance covered in any other number of minutes can be found by simple proportion.",
            "Revising ETAs during flight based on actual observed groundspeed, rather than solely relying on the pre-flight planned figure, is essential good practice, since actual wind conditions frequently differ from the forecast used for planning. If an aircraft covers a known distance in a different time than planned, the actual groundspeed can be calculated directly from that observed distance and time, and used to produce a more accurate ETA for the remainder of the flight.",

            "## WIND CORRECTION",
            "Wind correction calculations determine the heading a pilot must fly, and the resulting groundspeed achieved, to maintain a desired track over the ground in the presence of wind. The wind correction angle (WCA) is the angular difference between the aircraft's heading and its intended track, needed to counteract the sideways drift wind would otherwise cause.",
            "A useful approximation for wind correction angle: WCA (in degrees) is approximately equal to (crosswind component ÷ true airspeed) x 60. This approximation holds reasonably well for wind correction angles up to around 20-25°, beyond which the small-angle assumption underlying the approximation becomes progressively less accurate, though it remains a useful quick estimate for typical light aircraft crosswind situations.",
            "Once the wind correction angle has been determined, it is applied to the desired track to establish the required heading: a wind correction to the left (port) is subtracted from the track, while a wind correction to the right (starboard) is added to the track, in each case reflecting that the aircraft must point somewhat into the direction the wind is coming from to counteract drift and maintain the intended track over the ground. Groundspeed is then found by combining the headwind or tailwind component with the true airspeed, or more precisely using vector calculation methods where accuracy demands it.",

            "## FUEL CALCULATIONS",
            "Fuel calculations for a specific flight leg or the overall flight rely on multiplying the known fuel consumption rate (typically expressed in litres or gallons per hour) by the calculated flight time for that leg or flight. Where flight time is affected by wind (via its effect on groundspeed), an accurate fuel calculation depends on first correctly calculating the wind-affected groundspeed and resulting time, rather than simply using still-air time or distance alone.",
            "Endurance, the total time an aircraft can fly on its available fuel at a given consumption rate, is calculated as: Endurance = Fuel available ÷ Fuel flow rate. This calculation is used both for overall flight planning (ensuring sufficient endurance exists for the planned flight plus all required reserves) and for in-flight decision-making, such as determining how much additional time remains available before a diversion becomes fuel-critical.",
            "Combining fuel calculations with the standard components of a fuel plan, trip, taxi, contingency, alternate, and reserve, requires calculating each component's fuel requirement individually (using the appropriate time or distance for that specific component) before summing them to arrive at the total fuel required for the flight, a calculation that draws together virtually every other topic covered across flight planning and navigation into a single final, safety-critical figure.",
