        "## ATTENTION AND MEMORY",
        "Human attention is a genuinely limited resource, and the concept of divided attention describes the difficulty of effectively monitoring or performing multiple tasks simultaneously, a particular challenge in a cockpit environment where flying the aircraft, navigating, communicating, and monitoring systems all compete for the same limited attentional capacity. Attentional narrowing (sometimes called tunnelling) describes a tendency, particularly under high workload or stress, to focus excessively on one task or piece of information while neglecting others equally or more important, a well-documented factor in numerous accidents where a crew became fixated on a relatively minor issue while a more serious situation developed unnoticed.",
        "Memory in the context of human performance is generally described using three stages. Sensory memory holds raw sensory information for a very brief period (a fraction of a second), most of which is immediately discarded. Working (or short-term) memory holds a small amount of information actively being used or processed, typically only a handful of items at once, and is highly susceptible to being overwritten or lost if interrupted or distracted before the information is either used or transferred to longer-term storage. Long-term memory stores information for extended periods, potentially indefinitely, and includes both learned procedures and knowledge, as well as personal experiences.",
        "Working memory's limited capacity has direct, practical implications for cockpit procedures, this is precisely why checklists exist rather than relying purely on memory, and why interruptions during a critical procedure are a genuine hazard, since a pilot interrupted partway through a checklist or procedure may resume at the wrong point, or entirely omit a step, without any subjective awareness that anything has been missed.",

        "## STRESS",
        "Stress is the body's physiological and psychological response to a perceived demand or threat, and importantly, some degree of stress (sometimes called arousal) is not inherently harmful, and can actually improve performance up to a point by increasing alertness and focus. This relationship is often described by the Yerkes-Dodson law, or performance-arousal curve, an inverted U-shape in which performance is poor at very low arousal (under-stimulation, boredom, reduced vigilance), improves as arousal increases to an optimal moderate level, then progressively deteriorates again as arousal continues to increase beyond that optimal point into excessive stress.",
        "Stress can arise from multiple sources simultaneously, physiological (fatigue, illness, hunger), psychological (anxiety about a specific situation, personal life pressures), and environmental or situational (an actual in-flight emergency, time pressure, a difficult approach in poor weather), and these sources are frequently cumulative, meaning a pilot already carrying significant background stress from an unrelated source has a reduced capacity to effectively manage additional stress arising during a flight.",
        "Under excessive stress, cognitive performance characteristically narrows and degrades in recognisable ways: decision-making becomes less systematic and more reliant on oversimplified rules of thumb, attention narrows (as described above), and the ability to consider multiple options or gather additional information before deciding becomes significantly impaired, precisely when a more careful, considered response might genuinely be needed.",

        "## FATIGUE",
        "Fatigue is a state of reduced physical and mental performance capability resulting from prolonged exertion, inadequate rest, or disruption to normal sleep patterns, and its effects on cognitive performance, reaction time, judgement, and decision-making are well documented to be comparable to those of alcohol intoxication at a meaningful level of accumulated fatigue, despite fatigue carrying no equivalent legal blood-test limit.",
        "Acute fatigue results from a single period of inadequate rest or unusually demanding exertion, and is generally resolved by an adequate subsequent rest period. Chronic fatigue results from repeated or prolonged inadequate rest accumulated over an extended period (days or weeks), and is not fully resolved by a single good night's sleep, requiring a more sustained period of improved rest to genuinely recover from.",
