        "## HYPOXIA IN DEPTH AND TIME OF USEFUL CONSCIOUSNESS",
        "Building on the basic hypoxia concepts covered earlier, it is worth understanding the specific progression of symptoms in more detail, since recognising the early stages is precisely what allows a pilot to take corrective action before impairment becomes severe. The indifferent stage (from sea level to around 10,000ft for most individuals) involves minimal noticeable effect. The compensatory stage (roughly 10,000-15,000ft) involves the body's physiological compensation mechanisms, increased heart and breathing rate, beginning to show some signs of strain, though performance may still seem largely normal to the affected individual.",
        "The disturbance stage (roughly 15,000-20,000ft) involves genuinely noticeable impairment, including impaired judgement, memory, and coordination, though critically the affected individual frequently does not recognise this impairment in themselves, subjectively often feeling fine or even mildly euphoric. The critical stage (above approximately 20,000ft) involves severe impairment progressing rapidly to unconsciousness if not corrected, with very little time available for effective self-rescue.",
        "Time of Useful Consciousness figures are typically quoted for a seated, resting individual, and are significantly reduced by physical exertion (which increases the body's oxygen demand precisely when supply is already critically limited), by rapid decompression (compared to a slower, gradual pressure loss), and by pre-existing factors such as smoking or fatigue that already reduce a person's effective tolerance to reduced oxygen before the hypoxic event even begins.",

        "## TOXIC HAZARDS",
        "Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning is a particularly insidious cockpit hazard, since carbon monoxide is colourless, odourless, and non-irritating, giving no obvious sensory warning of its presence, and typically enters the cockpit through a cracked or degraded exhaust manifold or heat exchanger, contaminating the cabin heating system's air supply. Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin far more readily than oxygen does, effectively displacing oxygen from the bloodstream and producing symptoms very similar to hypoxia, headache, confusion, impaired judgement, drowsiness, that can be easily mistaken for simple fatigue or altitude-related hypoxia if the underlying cause is not recognised.",
