        "## ENGINE FAILURE",
        "An engine failure in flight requires an immediate, practised response, and the first and most critical action is establishing and maintaining the aircraft's best glide speed, the specific airspeed at which the aircraft achieves its maximum glide distance for a given altitude, since this maximises the time and distance available to select and reach a suitable forced landing area. Attempting to stretch a glide by flying slower than best glide speed is a genuinely dangerous and counterproductive response, since it increases drag and reduces, rather than increases, the actual distance achievable.",
        "Having established best glide speed, the pilot's immediate priority shifts to selecting a suitable field or open area for a forced landing, considering factors such as size, surface, slope, wind direction, and the presence of obstacles, and committing to a specific field reasonably early rather than continuing to search for a theoretically better option while valuable altitude and time are consumed in an ultimately indecisive search. Only once a suitable landing area has been identified and a plan established to reach it should the pilot's attention turn, time and altitude permitting, to attempting to identify and correct the cause of the failure, restart attempts if a restart appears feasible, and communication (a MAYDAY call including position and intentions).",
        "This priority sequence is often summarised as: aviate (fly the aircraft, establish best glide, select a field), navigate (manoeuvre toward the selected field), then communicate (make the MAYDAY call and complete any restart attempt or checklist actions), reflecting that flying the aircraft safely to the most survivable outcome always takes precedence over any other action, however important that other action might otherwise seem in isolation.",

        "## FIRE IN FLIGHT",
