        "## WAKE TURBULENCE",
        "Wake turbulence consists of a pair of counter-rotating vortices trailing from the wingtips of any aircraft generating lift, and is most hazardous behind larger, heavier aircraft, since the strength of the resulting vortices is directly related to the generating aircraft's weight, roughly proportional to weight and inversely related to airspeed and wingspan, meaning a heavy aircraft flying slowly (as during approach or departure) generates particularly strong wake vortices. Vortices are also generated behind lighter aircraft, and behind a helicopter in slow flight or a hover, where the vortex characteristics differ somewhat from a fixed-wing aircraft but remain a genuine hazard.",
        "Wake vortices tend to sink below the flight path of the generating aircraft, typically at a rate of a few hundred feet per minute initially, and drift with any crosswind component present, meaning the greatest hazard typically exists slightly below and, over time, downwind of the generating aircraft's actual flight path, rather than directly along it. Vortices persist longest in calm wind and stable atmospheric conditions, which do little to disperse or break up the rotating vortex structure, whereas turbulent or gusty conditions tend to break vortices apart more quickly.",
        "Avoiding wake turbulence requires appropriate positioning relative to a preceding, larger aircraft, when landing behind a larger departing or landing aircraft, planning to touch down beyond the point where the preceding aircraft rotated or touched down, and when taking off behind a larger aircraft, planning to lift off before the point where the preceding aircraft rotated, or delaying departure by an appropriate additional interval if unable to achieve this positioning, remaining above and, where practical, upwind of the preceding aircraft's flight path provides the most reliable practical protection.",

