        "A microburst is a particularly severe, localised form of windshear associated with a strong, concentrated downdraught from a thunderstorm or, less commonly, a vigorous shower cloud, which spreads out rapidly and violently upon reaching the surface, producing a rapid and severe change from headwind to tailwind (or vice versa) as an aircraft flies through the affected area, along with an initial strong downdraught component. An aircraft encountering a microburst on approach may experience a sudden airspeed increase (as it flies into the initial outflow headwind), followed rapidly by a severe airspeed decrease and downdraught (as it passes through to the outflow tailwind on the far side), a sequence that has caused serious accidents when not recognised and responded to immediately and aggressively.",
        "The appropriate response to an encountered or suspected microburst or severe windshear is an immediate, full-power go-around, since attempting to continue an approach through genuine microburst conditions risks a rapid, severe loss of airspeed and altitude from which recovery may not be possible given the limited altitude typically available during an approach. Recognising precursor indications, such as a virga (visible precipitation that evaporates before reaching the ground, often associated with a strong downdraught beneath it) or a specific pilot report of windshear from a preceding aircraft, and avoiding the affected area entirely where practical, is preferable to relying purely on recovery technique after an encounter has already begun.",

        "## ICING AND CONTAMINATION",
